course: books
lesson: 9
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter V, part 1: Advice from a Caterpillar
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Caterpillar and Alice stare at each other in silence until it slowly asks, "Who are you?", and Alice, after so many changes of size, honestly does not know.
explain: Every answer she gives, the Caterpillar contradicts in a few rude words, and when she asks who IT is, it just says "Why?", so she starts to walk away.
explain: It calls her back to tell her to keep her temper, then asks her to recite "You are old, Father William", which comes out as a crazy poem about a fat old man who stands on his head and balances an eel on his nose.
explain: "It is wrong from beginning to end," the Caterpillar says, and when Alice complains that three inches is a wretched height, it is offended, because it is exactly three inches tall itself.
explain: At last it crawls away, saying that one side of the mushroom will make her taller and the other side shorter: a prickly, puzzling talk that ends with a real clue.
fact: The Caterpillar speaks in "a languid, sleepy voice" ("languid" = slow and lazy) and asks "Who are you?"
fact: Alice answers "I—I hardly know, sir, just at present—at least I know who I was when I got up this morning, but I think I must have been changed several times since then."
fact: She says "I can't explain myself, I'm afraid, sir, because I'm not myself, you see"; "not myself" can mean feeling strange, and Alice also means it truly: she has changed.
fact: Alice says "being so many different sizes in a day is very confusing", and the Caterpillar answers "It isn't".
fact: The Caterpillar answers in very short words: "I don't see", "It isn't", "Not a bit", "Why?"
fact: Alice tells it that one day it will turn into a chrysalis (the hard case a caterpillar rests in) and then into a butterfly, which is true of real caterpillars.
fact: The Caterpillar's important advice is "Keep your temper", meaning stay calm and do not get angry.
fact: Alice says she tried "How doth the little busy bee" but "it all came different".
fact: In "You are old, Father William" a young man asks his father, in this order, why he stands on his head, how he turns a back-somersault, how he ate a whole goose with the bones and the beak, and how he balances an eel on his nose; the old man says his jaw got strong because he "argued each case with my wife".
fact: Father William's answers are silly: he no longer fears hurting his brain because he is "perfectly sure I have none", and he tries to sell his son an ointment at "one shilling the box" (a shilling was an old British coin).
fact: The poem ends: "I have answered three questions, and that is enough," "Be off, or I'll kick you down stairs!"
fact: Carroll's poem makes fun of a serious old poem that children had to learn by heart, in which a wise old man gives good advice.
fact: The Caterpillar says "It is wrong from beginning to end."
fact: Asked what size she wants to be, Alice first says "I'm not particular as to size", but adds that "one doesn't like changing so often".
fact: Alice says "three inches is such a wretched height to be" ("wretched" = miserable), and the Caterpillar, "exactly three inches high", says "It is a very good height indeed!"
fact: Alice thinks "I wish the creatures wouldn't be so easily offended!"; back in chapter III she said the same to the Mouse: "you're so easily offended, you know!"; the Caterpillar tells her "You'll get used to it in time."
fact: Leaving, the Caterpillar says "One side will make you grow taller, and the other side will make you grow shorter", and, as if hearing her thought, adds "Of the mushroom."
fact: Old words in today's English: "sternly" = strictly, "contemptuously" = with scorn, "give yourself airs" = act as if you are better than others, "supple" = bendy, "suet" = hard fat used in cooking.
exercise: What did the Caterpillar first say to Alice? | "Who are you?" | "Keep your temper." | "Go away at once!" | "What size are you?"
exercise: What kind of voice did the Caterpillar speak in? | A slow, sleepy voice | A loud and angry shout | A squeaky little voice | A cheerful singing voice
exercise: Why did Alice say she hardly knew who she was? | She had been changed several times since that morning | She had hit her head when she fell down the well | The Caterpillar had made her forget her own name | She had been asleep and was still half dreaming
exercise: Why did Alice say she could not explain herself? | Because she was not herself | Because she was too shy | Because she had no time | Because she had lost her voice
exercise: What did Alice say about being many sizes in a day? | It is very confusing | It is great fun | It is quite normal | It is very good for your health
exercise: What did the Caterpillar say to that? | "It isn't" | "I quite agree" | "Not yet, child" | "Of course"
exercise: What did Alice say the Caterpillar would turn into one day? | A chrysalis and then a butterfly | A moth and then a little bird | A worm and then a snake | A little frog and then a big green toad
exercise: What did the Caterpillar answer when Alice said it should tell her who it was first? | "Why?" | "Never!" | "Later." | "Who, me?"
exercise: What did Alice do then? | Turned away to leave | Sat down and cried | Ate the mushroom | Shouted crossly at it
exercise: What important thing did the Caterpillar call her back to say? | "Keep your temper." | "Who are you?" | "Eat the mushroom." | "Find the garden."
exercise: Which poem did Alice say had "all came different"? | "How doth the little busy bee" | "Twinkle, twinkle, little star" | "Mary had a little lamb" | "Humpty Dumpty sat on a wall"
exercise: Which poem did the Caterpillar ask her to repeat? | "You are old, Father William" | "How doth the little crocodile" | "Speak roughly to your little boy" | "The Owl and the Pussy-cat"
exercise: In the poem, why does Father William no longer worry about standing on his head? | He is sure he has no brain to hurt | His doctor said it was good for him | He wears a soft hat to protect it | He only does it when nobody looks
exercise: What does Father William try to sell his son? | Ointment at one shilling the box | A goose with its bones and beak | An eel to balance on his nose | A new pair of boots for him to wear at Christmas
exercise: How does Father William say his jaw got so strong? | By arguing every case with his wife | By eating a goose every day | By chewing suet all his life | By standing upside down on his head every day
exercise: What does Father William balance on the end of his nose? | An eel | A goose | A small box | An old shoe
exercise: How does the poem end? | "Be off, or I'll kick you down stairs!" | "Pray, what is the reason of that?" | "I do it again and again." | "Allow me to sell you a couple?"
exercise: What did the Caterpillar say about Alice's poem? | "It is wrong from beginning to end." | "That was beautifully said, my dear." | "Only the last verse was wrong." | "Say it again, but in French."
exercise: When asked what size she wanted to be, what did Alice say first? | She was not particular about size | She wanted to be as tall as a house | She wanted to be three inches tall | She wanted to be the Caterpillar's size
exercise: How tall was Alice at this time? | Three inches | Ten inches | Two feet | Nine feet and more
exercise: Why was the Caterpillar angry when Alice called three inches wretched? | It was exactly three inches high itself | It thought she was calling it a worm | It wanted her to be much smaller | It did not know what "wretched" meant
exercise: Here "wretched" means... | Miserable | Wonderful | Twisted | Tiny
exercise: What did the Caterpillar say Alice would do in time? | Get used to it | Grow very old and grey | Turn into a pig | Find the garden
exercise: What did the Caterpillar say one side would do? | Make her grow taller | Make her fall asleep | Make her sneeze | Make her invisible
exercise: One side of what? | The mushroom | The leaf | The pool | The thick wood
exercise: Here "Keep your temper" means... | Stay calm and do not get angry | Keep your thermometer in your pocket | Hold on tight to your hat in the wind | Remember what time it is today
exercise: Thinking back: which poem came out wrong in chapter II? | "How doth the little crocodile" | "You are old, Father William" | "Twinkle, twinkle, little bat" | "Speak roughly to your little boy"
exercise: Thinking back: in chapter III, who told her daughter "never to lose your temper"? | The old Crab | The Mouse | The Duck | The Lory, who was older
exercise: Thinking back: what did Alice see the Caterpillar doing at the end of chapter IV? | Smoking a hookah on top of a mushroom | Dancing round the thistle with the puppy | Eating a leaf at the bottom of a tree | Sleeping in the grass beside the path
test: Leo goes: "The Caterpillar was dead helpful, it explained everything nicely." Put him right. | It was rude and short, and only gave one clue at the end | It explained everything clearly from beginning to end | It said nothing at all and just went to sleep | It sang Alice a song to show her the right way
test: Ava, Gen Alpha: "The Caterpillar's replies were so dry, it ate though." ("ate" = did it brilliantly, in a cool way.) Which answer is NOT one of its short replies? | "Everybody has won" | "I don't see" | "Not a bit" | "Why?"
test: Why is "I can't explain myself, because I'm not myself" a clever joke? | "Not myself" means feeling odd, but Alice has truly changed | Alice has forgotten how to talk properly in English | The Caterpillar had told her not to talk about herself | She is pretending to be her sister Mabel from now on
test: What would have happened if Alice had walked away for good? | She would have missed the clue about the mushroom | She would have found the garden by herself at once | The Caterpillar would have chased her through the grass | She would have turned into a butterfly right away
test: Old Mr Jenkins, rough: "Kick you down the stairs, the cheek of it! Lad's asking for it." Who says that line in the poem? | Father William, to his son | The Caterpillar, to Alice | The son, to Father William | Alice, to the Caterpillar
test: True or false: Alice says the poem perfectly. | False: some words got altered, the Caterpillar says it's all wrong | True: the Caterpillar praised every single word | True: only the title was wrong, the rest was fine | False: she refused to say the poem at all
test: Why does Alice say "I wish the creatures wouldn't be so easily offended!"? | She keeps upsetting them without meaning to | All the creatures keep laughing at her | She wants them to be more offended | The Caterpillar told her a joke she did not understand at all
test: Mia from Melbourne: "Alice was heaps polite but the Caterpillar was a total drongo." ("heaps" = very, "drongo" = a fool or rude person.) Which shows Alice was polite? | She called it "sir" and kept answering kindly | She shouted at it and threw a stick at it | She never said one word to it the whole time | She laughed at its hookah and its blue colour
test: Why does the Caterpillar get angry about "three inches"? | Alice called its own height wretched | Alice said it was too big | Alice tried to measure it | Alice stepped on the end of its tail
test: Gran, from Edinburgh: "Yon beastie's no' very helpful, but it gave her a clue, ken." ("yon" = that, "beastie" = little creature.) What was the clue? | One side of the mushroom makes you taller, the other shorter | The lovely garden is behind the Rabbit's little house | Drinking from the pool of tears makes you grow | The Duchess has the golden key in her pocket
test: Looking back to chapter II: Alice said "Who in the world am I?" in the hall. Who asks her this question now? | The Caterpillar | The White Rabbit | The Mouse | The Dodo
test: Looking back to chapter III: whom else did Alice tell "you're so easily offended"? | The Mouse | The Dodo | The Lory | The Rabbit
test: Looking back: Alice has now been three inches tall. What else small has she been, from chapter I to now? | Ten inches, and a few inches in the pool | One inch only, and then two inches | Four inches, and five inches in the house | She has never been small before now
test: How has Alice's feeling about her own size changed since chapter I? | She is tired of changing and wants to stay one size | She now loves changing size every few minutes | She now wants to be as small as possible | She never thinks about her size any more
test: ★★★ Father William says "I have answered three questions". Count the son's questions in the poem (standing on his head, the somersault, the goose, the eel). How many did the son ask? | Four | Three | Two | Five
test: ★★★ So which question did Father William refuse to answer? | How he balanced an eel on his nose | Why he stood on his head | How he ate up the whole goose, bones and beak | Why he turned a somersault
test: ★★★ Read carefully: the Caterpillar says "Of the mushroom" though Alice only thought "One side of what?". What is odd about that? | It answered a question she never said aloud | It said it in French instead of English | It said it before she even saw the mushroom | It whispered it so quietly she could not hear
exam: Identify the question with which the Caterpillar commenced the conversation. | "Who are you?" | "What size are you?" | "Whence do you come?" | "Why do you weep?"
exam: Ascertain the counsel which the Caterpillar described as something important to say. | That the protagonist should keep her temper | That the protagonist should return home at once | That the protagonist should consume the mushroom | That the protagonist should locate the White Rabbit
exam: It is given that the Caterpillar was exactly three inches in height. Determine the reason for its displeasure at the protagonist's remark. | She had described that very height as wretched | She had described its hookah as unpleasant | She had declined to recite the requested verse | She had enquired as to its age and parentage
exam: Select the response most consistent with the established facts concerning the Caterpillar's final remark. | One side of the mushroom causes growth, the other diminution | Both sides of the mushroom cause the eater to fall asleep | The mushroom is poisonous and must on no account be eaten | The mushroom must be consumed whole to have any effect at all
